<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Merged into the generated bundle Info.plist by the Tauri bundler, which picks
  up any Info.plist sitting beside tauri.conf.json.

  These strings are the sentence macOS puts in the TCC prompt. Without them the
  prompt is a bare "tmuxy would like to access files in your Documents folder",
  which reads like the app is rummaging on its own — when in fact it is whatever
  the USER just ran in their shell. A terminal is an unusual case for TCC: the
  app is a conduit, so the copy says so.

  They change the prompt's WORDING, not how often it appears. Prompts repeating
  on every launch is a signing problem, not a plist one: TCC binds a grant to
  the app's designated requirement, and an ad-hoc signature identifies the app
  by a hash of its binary, so every build looks like a different app. A
  Developer ID signature (see the release workflow) is what makes a grant stick.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>NSDesktopFolderUsageDescription</key>
  <string>tmuxy runs your shell. Allow this so commands you run — ls, vim, git — can read and write files on your Desktop.</string>

  <key>NSDocumentsFolderUsageDescription</key>
  <string>tmuxy runs your shell. Allow this so commands you run — ls, vim, git — can read and write files in your Documents folder.</string>

  <key>NSDownloadsFolderUsageDescription</key>
  <string>tmuxy runs your shell. Allow this so commands you run — ls, vim, git — can read and write files in your Downloads folder.</string>

  <key>NSRemovableVolumesUsageDescription</key>
  <string>tmuxy runs your shell. Allow this so commands you run can read and write files on connected drives.</string>

  <key>NSNetworkVolumesUsageDescription</key>
  <string>tmuxy runs your shell. Allow this so commands you run can read and write files on network shares.</string>

  <!--
    Unlike the folder strings, this one decides WHETHER a prompt appears. Under
    the hardened runtime, an app without it (and without the apple-events
    entitlement in Entitlements.plist) has every Apple event a command sends
    refused on the spot (-1743), with no prompt: `osascript -e 'tell app
    "Mail" …'` in a tmuxy pane just fails, and the app never shows up under
    Privacy & Security → Automation to be allowed there either.
  -->
  <key>NSAppleEventsUsageDescription</key>
  <string>tmuxy runs your shell. Allow this so scripts and tools you run — osascript, Shortcuts, automation CLIs — can control this app.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
